/T (Introduction  Cybersecurity playsa vital role in protecting computer systems and networks from unauthorized access, cyberattacks, and data breaches. As organizations continue to rely  on digital technologies, the need for effective security monitoring and incident detection  has become increasingly important. Security Information and Event Management \(SIEM\) solutions help security teams monitor network activities, analyze security events, and respond to potential threats in real time.  During this week's practical lab, a complete virtual cybersecurity environment was  developed using VMware Workstation. The lab consisted of an Ubuntu virtual machine  configured as the Wazuh SIEM server, a Kali Linux virtual machine used for security testing,  and a Windows machine configured as a monitored endpoint. The objective of building this  environment was to understand how different systems communicate within a network,  how network traffic can be analyzed using Wireshark, and how Wazuh collects, monitors,  and analyzes security events from connected agents.  Throughout the practical exercises, virtual networking was configured, communication  between machines was verified, packet capturing was performed using Wireshark, and  Wazuh agents were successfully deployed on both Windows and Kali Linux. Finally, the  Wazuh dashboard was used to monitor endpoints, analyze generated security events, and  gain practical experience with basic Security Operations Center \(SOC\) activities.  This lab provided practical knowledge of network monitoring, packet analysis, endpoint  monitoring, and SIEM deployment, which are fundamental concepts in modern  cybersecurity operations.  Overview of Week 1  Thefirst week ofthe Cyberster internship was focused on setting up a complete virtual cybersecurity laboratory and understanding the fundamentals of network monitoring and Security Information and Event Management \(SIEM\). The primary objective was to build a secure lab environment where different operating systems could communicate with each other and generate security events for monitoring and analysis.  The practical work started with configuring VMware Workstation and creating the required  virtual machines. Ubuntu Linux was deployed as the central Wazuh SIEM server, Kali Linux  was prepared as the penetration testing machine, and a Windows system was configured  as the monitored endpoint. Virtual networking was established using NAT and Host-Only )
/E (Introduction  Cybersecurity playsa vital role in protecting computer systems and networks from unauthorized access, cyberattacks, and data breaches. As organizations continue to rely  on digital technologies, the need for effective security monitoring and incident detection  has become increasingly important. Security Information and Event Management \(SIEM\) solutions help security teams monitor network activities, analyze security events, and respond to potential threats in real time.  During this week's practical lab, a complete virtual cybersecurity environment was  developed using VMware Workstation. The lab consisted of an Ubuntu virtual machine  configured as the Wazuh SIEM server, a Kali Linux virtual machine used for security testing,  and a Windows machine configured as a monitored endpoint. The objective of building this  environment was to understand how different systems communicate within a network,  how network traffic can be analyzed using Wireshark, and how Wazuh collects, monitors,  and analyzes security events from connected agents.  Throughout the practical exercises, virtual networking was configured, communication  between machines was verified, packet capturing was performed using Wireshark, and  Wazuh agents were successfully deployed on both Windows and Kali Linux. Finally, the  Wazuh dashboard was used to monitor endpoints, analyze generated security events, and  gain practical experience with basic Security Operations Center \(SOC\) activities.  This lab provided practical knowledge of network monitoring, packet analysis, endpoint  monitoring, and SIEM deployment, which are fundamental concepts in modern  cybersecurity operations.  Overview of Week 1  Thefirst week ofthe Cyberster internship was focused on setting up a complete virtual cybersecurity laboratory and understanding the fundamentals of network monitoring and Security Information and Event Management \(SIEM\). The primary objective was to build a secure lab environment where different operating systems could communicate with each other and generate security events for monitoring and analysis.  The practical work started with configuring VMware Workstation and creating the required  virtual machines. Ubuntu Linux was deployed as the central Wazuh SIEM server, Kali Linux  was prepared as the penetration testing machine, and a Windows system was configured  as the monitored endpoint. Virtual networking was established using NAT and Host-Only )
