<<
/Title (Domain Name System \(DNS\)  The Domain Name System \(DNS\) is responsible for translating human-readable domain names into IP addresses. Before establishing communication with a remote server, a client first sends a DNS query to determine the corresponding IP address.  Observation:)
/Dest [24 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 1016 0 R
/Next 1012 0 R
>>
endobj
1015 0 obj
<<
/Title (since the Wazuh Manager must always remainreachable at a fixed address, the Host-Only interface was configured with a static IP.  A static IP address eliminates the possibility ofIPchanges after system reboot or network  restart. This is essential because the WindowsandKali Linux agents communicate with  the Wazuh server using its IP address. If the server'sIP changes, the agents would fail to  establish a connection until their configurationisupdated.  The network configuration file was modified usingUbuntu's Netplan utility. The Host-Only  interface \(ens34\) was assigned a permanent IPaddress, while the NAT interface \(ens33\)  continued to receive its address dynamically tomaintain internet connectivity.  After saving the configuration, the changes wereapplied successfully using the Netplan  configuration utility. The updated network settingswere verified using the ip a and ip  route commands, confirming that the static IPaddress had been assigned correctly and  that internet connectivity remained functional. The successful implementation of the static IPconfiguration ensured stable  communication between the Wazuh server andallconnected agents throughout the  laboratory exercises.  Checking Ip Route of Ubuntu)
/Dest [20 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 1013 0 R
/Next 1017 0 R
>>
endobj
1016 0 obj
<<
/Title (UDP packets were identified during DNS queries and other lightweight network services. The captured traffic demonstrated how UDP enables quick communication with minimal overhead.)
/Dest [24 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 1018 0 R
/Next 1014 0 R
>>
endobj
1017 0 obj
<<
/Title (Netplan Configuration Before Editing  The default Netplan configuration file was configured to obtain IP addresses dynamically  using DHCP for both network interfaces \(ens33 and ens34\). While this setup provided  network connectivity, it was not appropriate for a server that required a permanent IP  address.)
/Dest [20 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 1015 0 R
/Next 1019 0 R
>>
endobj
1018 0 obj
<<
/Title (User Datagram Protocol \(UDP\)  User Datagram Protocol \(UDP\) isaconnectionless transport layer protocol that provides faster communication by transmitting packets without establishing a connection. Unlike TCP, UDP does not guarantee packet delivery or sequencing, making it suitable for applications where speed is more important than reliability.  Observation:)
/Dest [24 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 1020 0 R
/Next 1016 0 R
>>
endobj
1019 0 obj
<<
/Title (Netplan Configuration After Editing  TheNetplanconfiguration filewas modified to assign a static IP address to the ens34 interface while keeping the ens33 interface configured for DHCP. This allowed the Ubuntu server to maintain internet access through the NAT network while providing a fixed IP address for communication with the Wazuh agents.)
/Dest [21 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 1017 0 R
/Next 1021 0 R
>>
endobj
